      '\n' +
      'The world advances one **game day** at a time. Wherever the game tells you how long something takes — a march, a building, a letter — it counts in game days, because that is the unit your decisions are made in.\n' +
      '\n' +
      "How much real time a game day takes is set per world. A test world can run faster than normal. The **date in the top bar** is the world's own calendar; the sun and moon beside it show the time of game day. **When something happens** uses your clock and date: **today 18:34**, **tomorrow 09:10**, or **Thu 09:00**; further away, a date is shown. **How long something lasts** stays in game days of the world: **food for 12 game days**, or **a 30-game day expedition**. Arrival estimates follow the world's current pace; they can change when the world pauses or its pace changes.\n" +
      '\n' +
      '## The world does not wait for you\n' +
      '\n' +
      "Everything keeps happening while you are away: production, eating, [[upkeep|pay]], marches, battles, the gods' moods. That is why the most useful skills in Megaron are the ones that work while you are absent — [[routes|standing orders]], well-filled [[food|food stores]], a [[stances|watch]] on the approaches — and why the [[coming-back|Notifications archive]] never throws anything away.\n" +
      '\n' +
      '## Before the world begins\n' +
      '\n' +
      `A new world's clock is frozen until enough Wanaxes have joined (see [[getting-in]]). You can look around while you wait, but no orders: building, recruiting, placing people, marching, founding your city, sending messengers — each is refused with *"The world has not begun"* and a count of how many Wanaxes have arrived. Give them once the world starts.\n`
    
  code: 'ERR_ASSERTION'
  name: 'AssertionError'
  expected:
  actual: |-
    ## game days and the clock
    
    The world advances one **game day** at a time. Wherever the game tells you how long something takes — a march, a building, a letter — it counts in game days, because that is the unit your decisions are made in.
    
    How much real time a game day takes is set per world. A test world can run faster than normal. The **date in the top bar** is the world's own calendar; the sun and moon beside it show the time of game day. **When something happens** uses your clock and date: **today 18:34**, **tomorrow 09:10**, or **Thu 09:00**; further away, a date is shown. **How long something lasts** stays in game days of the world: **food for 12 game days**, or **a 30-game day expedition**. Arrival estimates follow the world's current pace; they can change when the world pauses or its pace changes.
    
    ## The world does not wait for you
    
    Everything keeps happening while you are away: production, eating, [[upkeep|pay]], marches, battles, the gods' moods. That is why the most useful skills in Megaron are the ones that work while you are absent — [[routes|standing orders]], well-filled [[food|food stores]], a [[stances|watch]] on the approaches — and why the [[coming-back|Notifications archive]] never throws anything away.
    
    ## Before the world begins
    
    A new world's clock is frozen until enough Wanaxes have joined (see [[getting-in]]). You can look around while you wait, but no orders: building, recruiting, placing people, marching, founding your city, sending messengers — each is refused with *"The world has not begun"* and a count of how many Wanaxes have arrived. Give them once the world starts.
    
